# Nested-path access for Map<str, any> values produced by json.load
# (issue #1701, extended by #2398). Path is dot-separated str segments;
# missing intermediate or leaf returns None. Numeric segments resolve
# to List<any> int indexes when the intermediate any holds a List, or
# to str keys when it holds a Map (runtime tag dispatch).
#
# Path constraints (compile-time errors): the `path` argument must be
# a string literal — variable-bound paths are rejected. Empty paths
# (`""`), leading dots (`".a"`), trailing dots (`"a."`), and consecutive
# dots (`"a..b"`) are rejected as malformed.
#
# Numeric segments use base-10 parsing only: `"items.012"` resolves to
# index 12 (no octal handling). A leading '-' is accepted (#2398):
# `"items.-1"` is the last List element, `"items.-2"` the next-last,
# down to `"items.-len"` (the first). Negatives below `-len` and
# positives at-or-above `len` return None. The bare '-' segment (no
# digits after the sign) is not numeric and falls back to str-key
# lookup. On a Map intermediate, `-1` is the literal str key, not a
# reverse index.
#
# Keys containing '.' (or '\') are reached via backslash escape (#2398):
# `getPath(m, "a\\.b")` resolves the literal key `"a.b"`, and
# `getPath(m, "a\\\\b")` resolves `"a\b"`. The Ry string-literal layer
# halves user-source backslashes, so the user writes `"a\\.b"` for one
# literal backslash before the dot at codegen time. Unrecognised
# escapes (`\X` for any other X) and trailing bare backslashes are
# compile-time errors.
@public
@native
fn getPath(map: Map<str, any>, path: str) -> Option<any>

# Nested-path write for Map<str, any> (issue #1701, extended by #2398).
# Walks dot-separated str segments. Each intermediate must resolve to a
# Map<str, any> (str-key lookup) or a List<any> (int-index lookup with
# the same negative-wrap rules as getPath) at runtime; any other tag,
# a missing Map key, or an out-of-range List index raises a runtime
# error. The leaf segment is inserted (if new) or updated (if existing).
# Top-level receiver is COW-checked; intermediate Map / List values
# mutate in place, so callers sharing references to a nested container
# see the change.
#
# Path constraints (compile-time errors) match getPath: the `path`
# argument must be a string literal; empty / leading-dot / trailing-dot /
# consecutive-dot paths are rejected; the same backslash escape syntax
# applies (`\.` → literal '.', `\\` → literal '\').
#
# Leaf writes into a List index remain out of scope: the FINAL segment
# of the path is always a Map key write, so the immediately-enclosing
# container must be a Map at runtime. `setPath(m, "items.0", v)` where
# `items` is a List aborts with `intermediate segment 'items' is not a
# Map` — reach List elements via direct index assignment instead
# (`m["items"][0] = v`). Negative-index applies to List intermediates
# only (e.g. `setPath(m, "users.-1.name", "Alice")` is supported).
@public
@native
fn setPath(map: Map<str, any>, path: str, value: any) -> Unit
